WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:13.840
(Stream)

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.480
"I really want to try to become a pro CS player."

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.940
(Casting)

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:23.060
(Stream)

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.080
"Have you ever tried looking up, the male pornstars."

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.320
(Stream)

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.700
"People reported me for that are you serious?

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.060
Chat, you can't handle seeing a f*cking d*ck."

00:00:37.060 --> 00:01:03.320
(Stream)

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:15.120
As a kid, Jaryd “Summit1G” Lazar saw CS:GO for the first time while hanging around a

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.119
card shop.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.360
And the rest is history.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.840
"I used to go to this card shop, and one day I walked in and they had all these computers set up.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.400
They were playing Counter-Strike, and that was the first time I'd ever seen Counter-Strike

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.840
and I thought it was the coolest looking f*cking thing."

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:34.400
Summt1G has kept the monichor “1G” from a community forum he was apart of back in

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.400
the days of early Counter-Strike.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.960
"Summit was actually a jocker of 1G

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.340
But then because the actual 1G club stopped doing like scrimmages."

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.700
"Like died at the end of 1.6."

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:47.580
"It was like a scrim team in Counter-Strike called 1G and they brought Summit in at the end,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.740
and I think he just repped it for fun and on his brand people started doing the 1G thing,

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.780
and he's like yeah!"

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:58.420
Holding on so tightly to those two archaic characters is just so quintessential Summit.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.180
He’s the brash veteran.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.509
The no nonsense old-head who’s been here since the beginning and isn’t going to change

00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:06.240
his ways for anybody.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:17.240
(Stream)

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.400
And that’s why the CS community, the 1G
crew and those who’ve been Twitch fans from

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.200
the very beginning, love him so much.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.040
"Been watching you for a long time now, finally can give you some support love you."

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.360
"Yo Qutrix thanks man. I appreciate that dude, very much."

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.200
Since it’s been possible to stream CS — Summit1G has been doing it.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:47.600
(Stream)

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.960
Summit created his Twitch account on November 30th, 2011 and started streaming regularly

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.970
October of 2013.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:58.680
From the beginning, he’s always been all about the game.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.660
No matter what he’s playing, he’s constantly trying to get better and bringing you along

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.720
for the ride.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:12.760
(Casting)

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.480
Summit is widely recognized as one of the first to make it big as a streamer on Twitch

00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:22.639
and it’s not too hard to see his influence on the platform with a handful of top streamers

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.440
crediting him as one of the reasons they wanted
to get into streaming in the first place.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.120
"And the reason I started streaming, was because of Summit. I was watching Summit play

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:37.600
and back then it was like wordsy, how much he streamed inspired me what not he quit his job.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.360
I was like I wanted to do that."

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:44.920
Even in the early days of Twitch’s mainstream relevancy back in 2015, Summit was drawing

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.000
a large audience, even by today’s lofty standards.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.680
In August 2015 he averaged more than 16,000 viewers at a time.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.560
Since then he’s managed to hover around those numbers with outstanding consistency

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.840
— peaking at more than 28k in January of
2018.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:19.000
(Stream)

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.440
On stream, Summit’s kind of  just like you and me.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.320
He certainly wears his emotions on his sleeve and he's not afraid to full on tilt-off in front

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.800
of thousands of fans.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:37.480
(Stream)

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:42.760
Games play with our emotions, they pull us in and make us invested in our success.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:49.320
(Stream)

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.040
And nobody embodies that feeling of of being encapsulated more than Summit.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:57.400
He’s fiery, he appreciates a good meme and well…

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.920
"What would you do if there was a child right in front of you."

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.800
"I'd punch that kid in his little f*cking teeth."

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:08.320
During the infancy Counter Strike: Global Offensive’s, Summit made the jump from streamer

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.960
to competitive player.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.840
He had toyed with the idea of competing as he was beginning to stream but

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.320
committed to it in 2015.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.840
"I really wanna try to become a pro CS player."

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:24.400
"And I'm even willing to put in the time to get that done."

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:29.440
He played in tournaments with Torqued and Area 51 Gaming to middle-of-the-pack results,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.960
but to his ever-growing fan base, those results were mostly irrelevant.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:42.200
(Stream)

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.800
In a lot of ways his semi-pro career could never fail — just because of how nicely

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.080
it paired with his stream.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.760
He was constantly trying to improve and focus on gameplay — just like his viewers - which

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.600
made him more relatable than a lot of pros.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.240
"I'm still gonna obviously be the same streamer, you know I'm still gonna be talking to you guys a bunch,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.320
still gonna be thanking subs, thanking donations, having a chill time."

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.240
Think of it as a Twitch reality show where our protagonist Summit has quit his job and

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:10.870
is chasing the professional Counter Strike dream.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.520
And he’s taking his fans along for the ride.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.840
(Casting)

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.400
In addition to his ability to balance an urge to compete with a successful stream, Summit

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.280
has also been able to successfully juggle multiple games.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.880
Which is something that many streamers struggle with.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.000
Even in those early years Summit played DayZ and Rust along with CS:GO.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:41.560
(Stream)

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.200
As long as he was playing a shooter — people would show up and watch.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:07:04.080
(Stream)

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.520
Summit’s stream was steady, but his hunger for more competitive success had not subsided.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.640
So in 2016 he gave it another shot — this time with Splyce.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:23.440
During which, he would compete in the largest event of his pro career: DreamHack Austin in 2016.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:31.120
(Casting)

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.440
Austin was also the site of the most memorable moment of Summit’s professional career.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:41.600
A play that will go down in Counter-Strike history as one of the game’s biggest fails.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:57.560
(Casting)

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:01.820
Summit clutches the round for his team, but in retrospect his celebration was a little

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:07.040
premature as he walked directly into the flames of his own Molatov.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.540
Dying and handing over the round to CLG.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:12.720
As cringy as it may be to watch.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.220
This might just be the most quintessential “Summit” moment.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:19.360
"Now when you die to a molotov by yourself it's called pullin a Summit."

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.120
It’s the perfect intersection of his high skill level and at-times goofy persona.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:30.040
It’s the type of thing his chat would flame him for on-stream — and he did it in a Pro CS tournament.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.740
"Like when it happened I was like, "Dude my life's over."

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:39.440
Like I can't even step foot, in the world right now. Like I just... My life's over."

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.360
And what’s more is that sense that  Summit has owned his mistake.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:49.240
He’s wielded it, and it could be argued that he’s seen even more success as a streamer because of it.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:53.420
"Kind of like created a name for myself a little bit."

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:00.180
After his time with Splyce, Summit played with Mythic, a semi-pro CS:GO team filled

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:01.800
with popular streamers.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.930
His time with Mythic allowed him to scratch his itch for competitive play, but also stream

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:10.840
the games live on his channel giving him and his viewers the best of both worlds.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.600
(Casting)

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.080
As summer 2017 rolled around, Summit was hit by the same infatuation as almost every other

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:26.240
streamer and gamer in the FPS world — Player Unknown’s Battlegrounds.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:31.660
(Stream)

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.660
PUBG certainly flipped the script on streaming shooters.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:39.580
The pace of play is perfect and gives streamers ample time to interact with their chat between

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:40.240
games.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:55.180
(Casting)

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:57.360
The  whole experience is more like a television

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.080
show or movie rather than constant gameplay.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:07.200
(Stream)

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.480
Since Battle Royales have blown up, Summit has strayed away from competitive play.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.200
Just as big personalities like Shroud have been able to focus on being professional streamers

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.270
rather than professional gamers.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:23.720
A distinction that has become a lot more clear and plausible since Battle Royales have arrived.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.740
(Stream)

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:31.280
Summit 1G’s an old school gamer.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:35.380
He’s been around since the humble beginnings of both Twitch and esports.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:41.420
But even a guy with his feet as cemented into the scene as Summit isn’t immune to hate.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:43.800
As we saw this year:

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:50.480
(Stream)

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.780
Summit took major heat from his fanbase for

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:57.220
his choice to stream Fortnite with controversial Youtube star Jake Paul.

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.620
(Stream)

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:04.660
"Bro you're disappointed that a guy is playing a video game.

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:09.700
with another human being that you happen to not like from a YouTube series that you don't watch.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.360
"Alright? Relax."

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:13.860
At first glance, it may seem pretty tame.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:18.080
A harmless marketing move to collaborate with an incredibly famous person in the biggest

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.310
game in the world.

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:22.600
But, this is gaming — where loyalty is everything.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.420
(Stream)

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.440
"Ay man feel free, I understand completely."

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:33.640
So now that we’re here, and someone like Summit — the guy who quit his job to become

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:37.780
a streamer years ago — is streaming the new hot game with a millionaire clickbait

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:41.520
enthusiast — his fans really put him on blast.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:47.040
"If you're gonna look at it from a business perspective, playing with them. It ain't good for you.

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:49.680
like who gives a f*ck how many subs they have."

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.280
"Though your channel becomes completely cancer,

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:55.300
and your entire environment and channel becomes garbage."

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:59.060
"You know you're gaming with someone like just to game with them like it doesn't matter.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:09.520
"You know who you associate yourself with is very important."

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:15.280
And  then Summit reacted by doing a full stream without interacting with his chat at all.

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:18.960
"Everyone's jumping down my throat about playing some games with Jake Paul.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.440
and it's just like, what did you guys want me to do?"

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:24.320
"He DMs me and says, "Hey man your stream's dope dude."

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.160
What do you want me to be like, "Don't talk to me! I f*cking hate you you're a sh*tty human being."

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:32.880
Since that fateful April stream Summit’s viewership has taken a bit of a dip.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:38.529
He was averaging almost 20k viewers in April, dipped to just over 16k in May and then just

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.800
over 14k in July.

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:44.620
It took a long time for esports to be taken seriously in the mainstream.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.880
And it’s for that reason that its fans can be critical of their heros.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:53.800
They want consistency, absolute undenying passion and loyalty.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:57.480
"Oh esports ready, oh my f*cking goodness dude."

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:02.780
With that said, fans of Summit have historically been pretty hard on their favourite streamer.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:05.800
He often seems to be on the butt of their jokes.

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:10.560
Even back in 2016, Summit was swatted by his own chat

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:14.460
while mobile streaming himself playing Pokemon GO in a park.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.680
"I was holding my hands up, I wasn't gonna make a move. You know I wasn't going to go turn my stream

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:21.700
off with one hand you know. I was a little nervous, I mean you got guys pointing rifles at you,

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.540
they weren't pistols, they were rifles."

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:24.540
Recently, he 's

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:28.200
admitted that he’s developed a lot of anxiety around streaming

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:32.380
And that going live, has become a taxing and overwhelming process:

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:36.740
"Coming in here in front of you guys, it's the hardest thing I've had to do in a long time.

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:41.920
I know I've done this for years, and it just doesn't make sense to me why

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:48.520
why it's scary to get on, why it's scary to sit here in front of you guys. Why it's scary to talk."

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:57.320
"We're back. I'm hoping that we're here to stay, hopefully we don't have any f*cking problems. I'm feeling healthy."

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:27.400
(Stream)

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:31.440
The old-guard of esports needs somewhere to go and someone to flame.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.760
Somewhere to check out the new Call of Duty game or to get a nice daily dosage of streamer

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:36.440
salt.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:39.220
And Summit 1G will always be there for that.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:54.320
(Stream)

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:58.580
Summit1G isn’t the hero or the villain of the streaming world

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:01.500
He’s simply adapting the way he always has.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.880
"To be able to do 10k viewers, 9k viewers, 8k viewers,

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:09.900
playing any game, that's the f*cking dream right there bros."

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:13.840
"You don't need to be slamming 500k a month and sh*t dude.

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.860
to be happy, I'm very happy."

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.460
As we watch this world grow, there’s more pressure on people like Summit than anyone

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:24.540
else to play hot games like Fortnite or PUBG.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:29.260
To market themselves the way the likes of Ninja, Shroud and DrDisrespect have been able to.

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:34.340
"Some of you guys are ride or die 1Gs no matter what and I respect that."

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:39.320
To goof-off, to meme and be the five-tool streamer that viewers have come to expect.

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:48.380
(Stream)

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.280
Summit was there when it all started on Twitch.

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:56.390
And you better believe he’s going to scrap and claw to stick around now that all eyes

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:58.320
are on that purple icon.

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:01.280
Summit1G isn’t going anywhere.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:07.120
(Stream)

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:13.840
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

